{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the woman saw.</b> In her heart — like 'and I saw in my heart' (Ecclesiastes). Another interpretation: <b>and the woman saw</b> — transpose it: and the woman took of its fruit and ate, and saw that the tree was good for food, and gave also to her husband with her, and he ate." "<b>Also to her husband.</b> To include Cain and his twin sister, who had already been born, as we learned: they went up two and came down four." "<b>And she ate.</b> She ate first, thinking she was not included in the commandment. <b>And gave also to her husband</b> etc. — Rashi's explanation: lest she die and he live and marry another. That is, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to us, 'Be fruitful and multiply'; and whoever eats from the tree of knowledge — like me. If in her husband the blessing of 'be fruitful and multiply' is fulfilled, in me too it will be fulfilled."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "The final clause כמוני אם בבעלי is textually difficult; rendered hyper-literally. The logic appears to be the woman's reasoning that since she is like Elohim (having eaten), if the blessing of procreation is fulfilled through her husband, it will be fulfilled through her as well."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:02:40.440952523Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}